repl_replay — implementation plan
==================================

status: done (2026-07-09)

references I1..I5 (plans/repl_list). pass-through ("replay" => keys
["repl"]). companion to repl_edit and repl_rebase — the only way to
turn stale steps back into verified ones.

advertised metadata (the builtin table row)
-------------------------------------------

name          repl_replay
description   "Re-execute all stale steps in a REPL, in order, each
              from its predecessor's state. Steps become stale after
              repl_edit (the tail) or repl_rebase (all of them).
              Non-stale steps are not re-run. If a replayed step
              fails, replay stops there with the error. Replies with
              the number of steps replayed."
              (source: mcp_server.py replay :343, plus replay_repl
              semantics ir.ML:561.)
inputSchema   {"type": "object",
               "properties": {"repl": {"type": "string"}},
               "required": ["repl"]}
annotations   {"readOnlyHint": false, "idempotentHint": true,
               "openWorldHint": false}
              (idempotent in the success case: a second replay finds
              zero stale steps and is a no-op — "Replayed 0 stale
              steps". honest and useful for retry-happy clients.)
outputSchema  none.

name/argument mapping across the layers
---------------------------------------

  mcp client edge   tools/call "repl_replay", {"repl": "T"}
  scala builtin row backend.ir("replay", [(repl, T)])
  ml dispatcher     "replay" -> Ir.replay (get "repl")
  engine            ir.ML:574 — with_claim Op_Replay; replay_repl
                    re-executes stale steps under the repl's timeout;
                    out "Replayed N stale steps"

implementation steps
--------------------

1. [this tool] builtin row + handler; errors -> isError.
2. tests; rebuild per rules.

assumptions and how to test them
--------------------------------

T1  no stale steps -> ok no-op ("Replayed 0"), not an error.
    test [ml unit]: fresh repl with 2 clean steps; replay -> ok, 0.
T2  replay executes exactly the stale suffix and re-chains states:
    after edit 0 + replay, the states are consistent (state -1 equals
    a from-scratch run of the same script).
    test [ml unit]: 3 steps, edit 0 (equivalent text variant),
    replay; state -1 output equals the pre-edit state -1 output.
T3  failure mid-replay surfaces as a status error and leaves the
    already-replayed prefix clean, the failing-and-after steps stale
    — the repl is inspectable, not corrupted.
    test [ml unit]: make step 1 fail under the edited step 0 (edit
    changes a definition the later step needs); replay -> error;
    show -> step 1 (and 2) still [stale]; the repl still answers
    show/state.
    NOTE while implementing: verify what with_claim does with the
    partially-replayed repl on error — if the exception discards r',
    ALL steps stay stale (also fine); pin the actual behavior in the
    test and the description.
T4  replay respects the repl's timeout per step (exec_text carries
    #timeout r).
    test [ml unit]: timeout 1, make a stale step that sleeps ->
    replay errors with timeout.
T5  full chain.
    test [e2e]: edit-then-replay loop after repl_edit (shared
    fixture with plans/repl_edit T5).

