landed: done (2026-07-09). T1..T5 green at all layers. FIXTURE NOTE:
genuinely stale steps (as opposed to repl_edit's tail, which this
server's auto_replay=true default always replays immediately -- see
plans/repl_edit T3) cannot be produced via repl_edit here either, so
all ml-unit fixtures instead use repl_rebase: Ir.pin increments its
version on every call regardless of content change, so re-pinning a
REPL that a second REPL was init'd "pin@..." from is enough to make
Ir.rebase see a version mismatch and mark the dependent's steps stale
with no replay -- exactly what repl_replay needs to test against, and
requires no plans/repl_pin or plans/repl_rebase builtins to exist yet
(the ml-unit layer calls MCP_Repl.run "pin"/"rebase" directly against
the dispatcher). T3's open NOTE is now resolved: with_claim releases the
ORIGINAL claimed snapshot on exception (ir.ML:305), discarding whatever
replay_repl had rebuilt before raising, so a failed replay leaves ALL
previously-stale steps stale afterward -- not "prefix clean, tail
stale" as the plan's phrasing suggested, but a clean all-or-nothing
revert (verified empirically: 4 steps stale before a failing replay, 4
steps stale after). T4 confirmed replay honors the repl's CURRENT
timeout value, not whatever was in effect when the now-stale step first
succeeded. ml unit: mcp/Tools/HOL/Tests/MCP_Repl_Tests.thy; scala unit +
tools/call wiring: isabelle mcp_test; no new bridge test (no async
dimension of its own); e2e: mcp/test/test_mcp.py's test_repl_builtins
covers T1 (the idempotent no-op case) against a live server; T2-T4's
genuinely-stale-step scenarios need repl_pin/repl_rebase as builtins,
not yet implemented, so they stay at the ml-unit layer for now.
